**Read:** [Principles of Extractive Metallurgy — Tarun Kumar Kundu, NPTEL](../sources/Principles-of-Extractive-Metallurgy-NPTEL.lingua#r_7VCNEHN5VCQDDQTZ512PSHFZ78), the complete electrometallurgical theory; [Non-ferrous Extractive Metallurgy — Ray and Pugazhenthy, NPTEL](../sources/Nonferrous-Extractive-Metallurgy-NPTEL.lingua#r_29G1ZF58Y8EG123MNX3ZRNRE2W), all electrochemical extraction/refining material; [Environmental Degradation of Materials — Kallol Mondal, NPTEL](../sources/Environmental-Degradation-of-Materials-NPTEL.lingua#r_06WSRAYJJKWNTT146BKY5BN4QQ), the full relevant polarization and transport treatment.

After the shared Nernst step, define the electrode reaction, potential reference, current sign, surface state, local activities, and equilibrium potential. Overpotential measures departure from that equilibrium under stated conditions; it is not the same as the whole cell voltage or an uncompensated instrument reading.

Charge transfer, diffusion/migration/convection, nucleation/growth, adsorption, gas evolution, and changing surface area can contribute to measured polarization. A Butler–Volmer or Tafel relation has assumptions and a fitting domain. A straight region in a logarithmic plot is not sufficient to identify every elementary reaction or remove solution-resistance effects.

For a simple film model of a depositing ion, limiting current density can be written j_lim = nF k_m c_bulk as the surface concentration approaches zero, with a defined concentration basis and mass-transfer coefficient. Derive the relation from molar flux and charge. Changing mixing or geometry can change k_m; this is not an intrinsic universal current limit for a metal.

**Work:** reconstruct a complete electrode/polarization study with reference calibration, solution composition, resistance treatment, surface preparation/history, and transport evidence. Compare kinetic and transport explanations on independent conditions. Derive a limiting case, test units, and identify what additional observation could separate competing mechanisms. Explain consequences for useful deposit quality and competing reactions rather than treating current alone as production performance.

**Continue:** [Account for an electrolytic cell through electrochemical, electrical, and thermal terms](Cell-voltage-charge-energy-heat-and-geometry.lingua#r_6Y3CD4F9VN7TNTJ0MVKS5ABK3K).

**Return:** [Metals and metallurgical production — feed, refining, structure, useful products, and recovery](../Metals-and-metallurgical-production.lingua#r_59BDAQXT81552YF5F8TH4KQQH1).
